// Marks a type as "numeric" for defaulting/constraint purposes — see
// cleave/src/infer.rs's module docs on the numeric-literal gap this exists
// to eventually close (a literal's type variable should check membership
// here instead of defaulting blindly, regardless of what it got unified
// with along the way).
//
// No axioms/methods required — a pure existence-marker algebra. The
// simplest possible real (not fabricated-for-a-test) stdlib content:
// `Registry::has_impl("Num", ...)` is all that's needed for now.
algebra Num<T> {}

// `Int`/`Float` split `Num` along the one distinction a bare numeric
// literal's own text already carries (`1` vs `1.0`) — the language has no
// implicit conversions anywhere, integers and floats included, so a literal
// written with a `.` genuinely cannot become an integer type and vice
// versa; this is what makes that a real, checkable constraint rather than a
// defaulting-only hint. Deliberately two *independent* marker algebras, not
// `Int<T>: Num` / `Float<T>: Num` (algebra-bound inheritance isn't
// implemented anywhere in the registry/inference yet — `bounds` is parsed
// and merged across files, never consulted for `has_impl_named` — so a type
// implementing `Int` isn't automatically treated as implementing `Num`
// through this alone). A literal's own constraint set names all three
// explicitly (`Num` *and* `Int`, or `Num` *and* `Float`) rather than relying
// on inheritance to fill in `Num` — see `infer.rs`'s `NumberLit` handling.
algebra Int<T> {}
algebra Float<T> {}

impl Num<i8> {}
impl Num<i16> {}
impl Num<i32> {}
impl Num<i64> {}
impl Num<f32> {}
impl Num<f64> {}

impl Int<i8> {}
impl Int<i16> {}
impl Int<i32> {}
impl Int<i64> {}

impl Float<f32> {}
impl Float<f64> {}
